([PROBLEM 1: Subnetting a Class C Network into 4 Subnets]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Statement: An academic department is assigned the Class C network address) Tj
T*
(192.168.10.0/24. Subnet this network into 4 equal subnets. For each subnet, determine:) Tj
T*
(\(1\) Subnet Mask in dotted-decimal and CIDR notation, \(2\) Subnet Network ID, \(3\) Usable) Tj
T*
(Host IP Range, and \(4\) Directed Broadcast Address.) Tj
T*
(Step-by-Step Mathematical Derivation:) Tj
T*
(* 1. Determine Borrowed Bits \(b\):) Tj
T*
(- Number of required subnets = 4 -> 2^b >= 4 -> b = 2 bits.) Tj
T*
(* 2. Calculate New Subnet Mask:) Tj
T*
(- Original mask = /24 \(24 bits\). New mask = 24 + 2 = /26 bits.) Tj
T*
(- 4th octet binary = 11000000_2 = 128 + 64 = 192.) Tj
T*
(- Subnet Mask = 255.255.255.192 \(/26\).) Tj
T*
(* 3. Calculate Block Size \(Delta\):) Tj
T*
(- Remaining host bits h = 8 - 2 = 6 bits. Block size = 2^6 = 64 \(or 256 - 192 = 64\).) Tj
T*
(* 4. Detailed Subnet Allocation Table:) Tj
T*
(- Subnet 1: Network ID = 192.168.10.0/26 | Usable Range: 192.168.10.1 to 192.168.10.62 |) Tj
T*
(Broadcast: 192.168.10.63.) Tj
T*
(- Subnet 2: Network ID = 192.168.10.64/26 | Usable Range: 192.168.10.65 to) Tj
T*
(192.168.10.126 | Broadcast: 192.168.10.127.) Tj
T*
(- Subnet 3: Network ID = 192.168.10.128/26 | Usable Range: 192.168.10.129 to) Tj
T*
(192.168.10.190 | Broadcast: 192.168.10.191.) Tj
T*
(- Subnet 4: Network ID = 192.168.10.192/26 | Usable Range: 192.168.10.193 to) Tj
T*
(192.168.10.254 | Broadcast: 192.168.10.255.) Tj
T*
(* 5. Verification: Total usable hosts = 4 x 62 = 248 hosts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([PROBLEM 2: Variable Length Subnet Masking \(VLSM\) Enterprise Design]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Statement: Design an optimal VLSM address plan for network 172.16.0.0/22 for) Tj
T*
(four departments with the following host requirements: Engineering \(400 hosts\), Sales) Tj
T*
(\(120 hosts\), Admin \(50 hosts\), and WAN Point-to-Point Link \(2 hosts\).) Tj
T*
(Step-by-Step VLSM Allocation Protocol \(Highest to Lowest\):) Tj
T*
